    <section class="section" id="how-to-play">
      <h2>How to Play Murder Sudoku</h2>
      <p>Murder sudoku is a hybrid of two classics: the row-and-column discipline of sudoku and the deduction of a whodunit. Each case gives you a mansion floor plan carved into named rooms, a cast of suspects, and a set of witness statements. Every statement is true — your job is to work out where each suspect was standing when the body was found.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Place the suspects.</strong> Select a suspect from the tray, then tap a room cell to place them. No two figures can share a row or a column — the same sudoku rule you already know.</li>
        <li><strong>Read the statements.</strong> Each witness gives exactly one testimony: where they were, what they were standing on, who they were with, or which room was empty. All of them are true.</li>
        <li><strong>Find the killer.</strong> The suspect who ends up alone in the room with the victim is the murderer. When every suspect is placed, hit <strong>Accuse!</strong> to close the case.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Start with a <strong>4×4 Easy</strong> case to learn the flow of deduction, then move up to <strong>6×6 Tricky</strong> for longer chains of reasoning.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="tips">
      <h2>Deduction Tips</h2>
      <h3>Start with the room statements</h3>
      <p>Statements like <em>"Mochi was in the Library"</em> or <em>"The Cellar was locked — nobody inside"</em> eliminate the most cells. Place what you know first, and the row and column rule will squeeze the remaining suspects into fewer and fewer legal cells.</p>
      <h3>Use the sudoku rule aggressively</h3>
      <p>Because no two figures share a row or column, every placement immediately eliminates an entire row and column. A suspect pinned to one room often has only one legal cell left once the neighbors are placed.</p>
      <h3>Watch the victim's room</h3>
      <p>The room holding the victim contains exactly two figures: the victim and the killer. Room-count statements about that room are usually the fastest route to identifying the murderer.</p>
    </section>
